# px-run executes a single test run. It owns a single dedicated run directory under PX_OUT and
# writes its own files exclusively within that directory, while backend state and the
# planned-action record are stored in the shared state directory under PX_OUT.
#
# Execution proceeds through five ordered steps:
#   1. Resolve the configuration, mint the unique run identifier, and create the run directory.
#   2. Capture the pre-run snapshot of the cluster through the substrate contract.
#   3. Start the workload driver and inject the disruptions defined by the schedule.
#   4. Capture the post-run snapshot and compare each instance against its pre-run state and
#      restart count.
#   5. Report a single exit code from the shared exit-code vocabulary.
#
# Because the driver owns the verdict for its workload, its exit status passes through unchanged
# unless overridden by a cluster anomaly: an unplanned instance change replaces an exit code of 0
# or 1 with the cluster-anomaly exit code 3. An instance is considered changed if its restart count
# rose, if a running instance exited or transitioned to unknown, or if the instance vanished. A
# change is unplanned when no planned action recorded during the run covers that instance, showing
# that the failure originated in the cluster rather than the workload so that callers can tell the
# two apart.
#
# Because every cluster query and fault injection goes through the substrate contract, this script
# references no specific container runtime and executes unchanged across every supported backend.
#
# Run record format: A real run writes a single flat tab-separated record file at
# $PX_RUN_DIR/run-record.tsv whose first column identifies the row type. Because a subsequent
# development round defines the final bundle layout, this file is intentionally structured as a
# single flat stream that a bundle writer can ingest without walking a directory tree:
#   run       run_id run_class backend scope driver
#   stage     name ts_ms
#   version   phase component version
#   instance  phase <the instance schema of the contract>
#   restart   phase role index count
#   sample    phase <the sample schema of the contract>
#   disrupt   ordinal when_kind when_value action role index argument state
#   action    ts_ms action role index argument state exit_code
#   phase     role index <the phase schema of the contract>
#   diff      role index before after verdict pre_state post_state
#   term      role index <the term schema of the contract>
#   oom       role index <the oom schema of the contract>
#   driver    elapsed_ms exit_code log
#   skip      phase subject reason
#   result    exit_code name anomaly
# Each diff row carries a verdict of unchanged, planned, unplanned, vanished, or unknown. Every
# unplanned or vanished instance is also accompanied by a term row and an oom row containing
# evidence of the underlying cause. A record file that ends without a result row indicates an
# interrupted run.
#
# A dry run neither creates a run directory nor invokes the backend; instead, it writes the same
# row stream to stdout and emits "plan step detail" rows describing what a real run would execute.

set -euo pipefail

SELF="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
# Sourcing the backend contract automatically loads lib/common.sh as well.
# shellcheck source=SCRIPTDIR/../lib/backend/contract.sh
. "$SELF/../lib/backend/contract.sh"

px_trap_enable

## @description  Prints the command-line usage, disruption specification grammar, and exit codes.
## @return       Writes the usage text to stdout. Exit 0.
usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage: px-run [option...] [-- driver argument...]

Runs one test. px-run owns one run directory, runs one driver, snapshots the
cluster before and after the driver, injects the declared disruptions, and
reports one exit code.

Options:
  -d, --driver NAME|PATH  the driver program: a path, or a name that resolves
                          to <drivers>/NAME/driver.sh. A real run needs one.
  -s, --scope ID          the scope id of a provisioned cluster, default
                          PX_SCOPE_ID, else the scope px-cluster recorded for
                          PX_CLUSTER_ID. Without one px-run takes no snapshot
                          and refuses a disruption.
  -b, --backend NAME      the substrate backend to bind
  -c, --class NAME        the run class, which selects the configuration
  -D, --disrupt SPEC      one disruption, repeatable. Several specifications
                          in one value are separated by a semicolon.
  -t, --timeout SECONDS   the bound on the driver, default PX_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT
  -e, --set NAME=VALUE    one configuration value, repeatable
  -f, --config FILE       one configuration file, repeatable
  -n, --dry-run           print the plan, call no backend, make no run
                          directory
  -k, --keep              keep temporary files, and give the switch to the
                          driver
  -v, --verbose           report progress in detail
  -h, --help              print this text

A disruption specification is WHEN,ACTION,ROLE,INDEX[,ARGUMENT].
  WHEN      +SECONDS after the driver starts, or phase:NAME
  ACTION    kill, signal or restart
  ARGUMENT  the kill mode (hard or delete, default hard), the signal name, or
            empty for restart
A phase name is a point that px-run announces: setup, snapshot_pre,
driver_start, driver_exit and snapshot_post. A specification that never fires
is recorded as skipped. signal and restart are optional substrate functions,
so a backend that lacks one records a skip instead of a failure.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 failure, 2 usage, 3 cluster anomaly, 4 timeout,
5 unsupported, 6 no such object. A status of 0 to 6 from the driver passes
through unchanged. An unplanned restart, an unplanned exit of a running
instance, or an instance that vanished replaces 0 or 1 with 3. A planned
action of this run, or of px-cluster during this run, explains a change.

Output: a real run writes the run directory to stdout and its record to
run-record.tsv in that directory. A dry run writes the plan to stdout.
EOF
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global runtime state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leaving RECORD_FILE empty directs all record output to stdout, allowing dry-run mode to print the
# execution plan without creating any files on disk.

RECORD_FILE=""
RUN_ID=""
RUN_DIR=""
SCOPE=""
DRIVER_SPEC=""
DRIVER_PATH=""
DRIVER_LOG=""
DRIVER_PID=""
DRIVER_STATUS=0
DRIVER_START_S=0
DRIVER_START_MS=0
DRIVER_DEADLINE=0
DRIVER_TIMEOUT=0
DRIVER_TIMED_OUT=0
SNAPSHOT_FAILED=0
ANOMALY=0
PRE_RESTARTS=""
POST_RESTARTS=""
PLANNED_SINCE_MS=0
DISRUPT_COUNT=0
DISRUPT_KIND=()
DISRUPT_WHEN=()
DISRUPT_ACTION=()
DISRUPT_ROLE=()
DISRUPT_INDEX=()
DISRUPT_ARG=()
DISRUPT_STATE=()
DRIVER_ARGS=()
DRIVER_CMD=()
CONFIG_FILES=()
SET_PAIRS=()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run record emission
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Writes the formatted row to the active record file, or to stdout during a dry run. Fails if any
# field contains an embedded tab or newline character.
## @description  Formats and writes a single tab-separated record row from individual field values.
## @param        field...
## @return       Writes to the record file or stdout. Exit 0, or 1 if a field holds a tab/newline.
record_row() {
  if [ -n "$RECORD_FILE" ]; then
    px_tsv_row "$@" >>"$RECORD_FILE"
  else
    px_tsv_row "$@"
  fi
}

# Rows returned by substrate contract calls already contain tab-delimited fields in their canonical
# schema order, so callers prepend the record prefix columns and pass the resulting line here.
## @description  Writes a pre-formatted tab-separated record line to the active output stream.
## @param        text
## @return       Writes the line to the record file, or to stdout during a dry run. Exit 0.
record_line() {
  if [ -n "$RECORD_FILE" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$1" >>"$RECORD_FILE"
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$1"
  fi
}

# Logs a stage transition in the run record and triggers any pending phase-based disruptions
# scheduled for that point. Future implementation rounds will announce driver verb phases through
# this same entry point so that phase triggers require no additional dispatch mechanism.
## @description  Records arrival at a named execution phase and fires matching disruptions.
## @param        name
## @return       Exit 0.
phase_reached() {
  local name=$1 index=0
  record_row stage "$name" "$(px_epoch_ms)"
  while [ "$index" -lt "$DISRUPT_COUNT" ]; do
    if [ "${DISRUPT_STATE[$index]}" = pending ] &&
      [ "${DISRUPT_KIND[$index]}" = phase ] &&
      [ "${DISRUPT_WHEN[$index]}" = "$name" ]; then
      disrupt_fire "$index"
    fi
    index=$((index + 1))
  done
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workload driver lifecycle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Values containing a slash are treated as explicit filesystem paths, whereas bare names are
# resolved beneath the configured drivers directory according to the fixed harness directory layout.
## @description  Resolves a driver name or path specification to an absolute executable path.
## @param        name_or_path
## @return       Writes the absolute path to stdout. Exit 0, or 2 if absent or not executable.
driver_resolve() {
  local spec=$1 dir path
  case "$spec" in
  */*) path=$spec ;;
  *)
    dir=${PX_DRIVERS_DIR:-drivers}
    case "$dir" in
    /*) ;;
    *) dir="$PX_HOME/$dir" ;;
    esac
    path="$dir/$spec/driver.sh"
    ;;
  esac
  if [ ! -f "$path" ]; then
    px_die "$PX_EXIT_USAGE" "no driver program at $path"
  fi
  if [ ! -x "$path" ]; then
    px_die "$PX_EXIT_USAGE" "the driver program is not executable: $path"
  fi
  px_realpath "$path" || px_die "$PX_EXIT_FAIL" "cannot resolve $path"
}

# Centralizes construction of the driver invocation command line so that when a subsequent round
# introduces the six-verb driver protocol, the verb argument can be prepended ahead of the operator
# arguments in this single location without requiring changes to any caller.
## @description  Assembles the command-line array for a single driver invocation in DRIVER_CMD.
## @return       Sets DRIVER_CMD. Exit 0.
driver_command() {
  # Under set -u, Bash 3.2 treats an empty array expansion as an unbound variable error, so the
  # operator arguments are expanded conditionally.
  DRIVER_CMD=("$DRIVER_PATH" ${DRIVER_ARGS[@]+"${DRIVER_ARGS[@]}"})
}

# The driver executes asynchronously in the background so that px-run remains free to inject timed
# disruptions while the workload is active. All driver output is redirected to a log file because
# the standard output of px-run serves as a structured data channel.
## @description  Launches the workload driver in the background and computes its timeout deadline.
## @return       Sets DRIVER_PID, DRIVER_START_S, DRIVER_START_MS, and DRIVER_DEADLINE. Exit 0.
driver_start() {
  driver_command
  # Using exec replaces the background subshell with the driver binary itself, ensuring that the
  # recorded PID refers directly to the driver rather than an intermediate shell and that signals
  # reach the workload directly. Any child processes spawned by the driver are owned by the driver,
  # which is required by the driver contract to handle termination signals cleanly.
  ( exec "${DRIVER_CMD[@]}" ) >"$DRIVER_LOG" 2>&1 &
  DRIVER_PID=$!
  DRIVER_START_S="$(px_epoch_s)"
  DRIVER_START_MS="$(px_epoch_ms)"
  DRIVER_DEADLINE=$((DRIVER_START_S + DRIVER_TIMEOUT))
  px_info "driver $DRIVER_PATH started as process $DRIVER_PID"
}

# Requests graceful termination via SIGTERM first and escalates to SIGKILL after 10 seconds, giving
# the driver time to flush its output while ensuring unresponsive processes are still terminated.
# Registered as an exit hook so that an interrupted px-run invocation never leaves an orphaned
# workload process running behind.
## @description  Terminates the active background driver process gracefully, then forcefully.
## @return       Exit 0.
driver_terminate() {
  local waited=0
  [ -n "$DRIVER_PID" ] || return 0
  kill -0 "$DRIVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  px_warn "stopping driver process $DRIVER_PID"
  # Suppress the shell's default job-termination notification on stderr, which would expose the
  # raw internal command line after the harness has already logged a structured warning message.
  {
    kill -s TERM "$DRIVER_PID" || return 0
    while [ "$waited" -lt 10 ] && kill -0 "$DRIVER_PID"; do
      sleep 1
      waited=$((waited + 1))
    done
    if kill -0 "$DRIVER_PID"; then
      kill -s KILL "$DRIVER_PID" || true
    fi
  } 2>/dev/null
}

# Periodic polling allows a single process to monitor the child driver and dispatch offset-based
# disruptions on schedule without spawning a separate scheduler process.
## @description  Monitors the running driver until completion or timeout and fires due disruptions.
## @return       Sets DRIVER_TIMED_OUT if the timeout bound expires. Exit 0.
driver_watch() {
  local interval now
  interval=${PX_POLL_INTERVAL_S:-1}
  while kill -0 "$DRIVER_PID" 2>/dev/null; do
    now="$(px_epoch_s)"
    if [ "$now" -ge "$DRIVER_DEADLINE" ]; then
      DRIVER_TIMED_OUT=1
      px_error "the driver passed its bound of ${DRIVER_TIMEOUT}s"
      driver_terminate
      return 0
    fi
    disrupt_due "$now"
    sleep "$interval"
  done
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Planned fault accounting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Instance state changes or restarts caused by a fault injected by px-run or an administrative
# action performed by px-cluster during the run are considered expected. The post-run comparison
# relies on this distinction because only unexplained changes constitute a cluster anomaly. Both
# tools append to the substrate contract's shared planned-action record, and the comparison logic
# consults only entries recorded during the current run.

# The inspection window opens immediately before the pre-run snapshot so that actions from earlier
# runs cannot excuse changes in the current run. If the planned-action record cannot be read, no
# instance is treated as covered, preventing a read error from masking a genuine cluster fault.
## @description  Checks whether a planned action recorded during this run covers the instance.
## @param        role, index
## @return       Exit 0 if a planned action covers the instance, or 1 otherwise.
planned_covers() {
  local status=0
  sb_planned_contains "$SCOPE" "$1" "$2" "$PLANNED_SINCE_MS" || status=$?
  case "$status" in
  0) return 0 ;;
  1) return 1 ;;
  esac
  px_warn "cannot read the planned-action record, exit $status:" \
    "a change of $1/$2 counts as unplanned"
  return 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster snapshots and anomaly detection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## @description  Looks up the recorded state and restart count of an instance in a snapshot list.
## @param        list (the lines of one snapshot), role, index
## @return       Writes the state, a tab, and the count to stdout. Exit 0, or 6 if not found.
snapshot_lookup() {
  local list=$1 role=$2 index=$3 line
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -n "$line" ] || continue
    case "$line" in
    "$role$PX_TAB$index$PX_TAB"*)
      printf '%s\n' "${line#"$role$PX_TAB$index$PX_TAB"}"
      return 0
      ;;
    esac
  done <<EOF
$list
EOF
  return "$PX_EXIT_NO_OBJECT"
}

# Captures substrate tooling versions for inclusion in the diagnostic bundle manifest so that test
# results remain attributable to the exact software versions that produced them.
## @description  Queries and records the versions of the underlying substrate tooling.
## @param        phase
## @return       Exit 0.
snapshot_versions() {
  local phase=$1 rows row status=0
  rows="$(sb_call sb_version_info)" || status=$?
  if [ "$status" != 0 ]; then
    record_row skip "$phase" version_info "exit $status"
    return 0
  fi
  while IFS= read -r row; do
    [ -n "$row" ] || continue
    record_line "version$PX_TAB$phase$PX_TAB$row"
  done <<EOF
$rows
EOF
}

# Resource sampling is an optional substrate capability; if the active backend does not declare a
# metrics source, a skip entry is recorded instead of failing the snapshot.
## @description  Captures and records a single resource-utilization sample for every instance.
## @param        phase
## @return       Exit 0.
snapshot_samples() {
  local phase=$1 rows row status=0
  if ! sb_capability resource_sampling >/dev/null; then
    record_row skip "$phase" resource_sampling "the backend declares no metrics source"
    return 0
  fi
  rows="$(sb_call sb_sample_resources "$SCOPE")" || status=$?
  if [ "$status" != 0 ]; then
    record_row skip "$phase" sample_resources "exit $status"
    return 0
  fi
  while IFS= read -r row; do
    [ -n "$row" ] || continue
    record_line "sample$PX_TAB$phase$PX_TAB$row"
  done <<EOF
$rows
EOF
}

# Collects the instance inventory, per-instance state and restart counts, substrate tool versions,
# and a resource sample. Because instance states and restart counts provide the baseline evidence
# required for cluster anomaly detection, an unreadable inventory is treated as a hard failure
# rather than a skippable operation.
## @description  Captures a full cluster snapshot for the specified execution phase.
## @param        phase (pre or post)
## @return       Exit 0, or 1 if the cluster inventory cannot be read.
snapshot_take() {
  local phase=$1 rows row role index state count list="" status=0
  local role_col index_col state_col
  role_col="$(sb_schema_index instance role)"
  index_col="$(sb_schema_index instance index)"
  state_col="$(sb_schema_index instance state)"
  rows="$(sb_call sb_list "$SCOPE")" || status=$?
  if [ "$status" != 0 ]; then
    px_error "the $phase snapshot cannot read the inventory of scope $SCOPE"
    record_row skip "$phase" inventory "exit $status"
    return "$PX_EXIT_FAIL"
  fi
  while IFS= read -r row; do
    [ -n "$row" ] || continue
    record_line "instance$PX_TAB$phase$PX_TAB$row"
    role="$(px_tsv_field "$role_col" "$row")" || continue
    index="$(px_tsv_field "$index_col" "$row")" || continue
    state="$(px_tsv_field "$state_col" "$row")" || state=unknown
    status=0
    count="$(sb_call sb_restart_count "$SCOPE" "$role" "$index")" || status=$?
    if [ "$status" != 0 ]; then
      px_warn "no restart count for $role/$index, exit $status"
      count=""
    fi
    record_row restart "$phase" "$role" "$index" "$count"
    list="$list$role$PX_TAB$index$PX_TAB$state$PX_TAB$count$PX_NL"
  done <<EOF
$rows
EOF
  if [ "$phase" = pre ]; then
    PRE_RESTARTS=$list
  else
    POST_RESTARTS=$list
  fi
  snapshot_versions "$phase"
  snapshot_samples "$phase"
  return 0
}

# Queries the backend for the instance's termination reason and out-of-memory kill status. Because a
# vanished instance can provide neither, any query that the backend refuses is logged as a skip row
# in the run record.
## @description  Records termination and out-of-memory evidence for an anomalous cluster instance.
## @param        role, index
## @return       Exit 0.
evidence_record() {
  local role=$1 index=$2 term="" oom="" reason="" kill="" status=0 column
  term="$(sb_call sb_termination_reason "$SCOPE" "$role" "$index" current)" ||
    status=$?
  if [ "$status" = 0 ] && [ -n "$term" ]; then
    record_line "term$PX_TAB$role$PX_TAB$index$PX_TAB$term"
    column="$(sb_schema_index term reason)" || column=1
    reason="$(px_tsv_field "$column" "$term")" || reason=""
  else
    record_row skip post "termination reason of $role/$index" "exit $status"
  fi
  status=0
  oom="$(sb_call sb_oom_killed "$SCOPE" "$role" "$index")" || status=$?
  if [ "$status" = 0 ] && [ -n "$oom" ]; then
    record_line "oom$PX_TAB$role$PX_TAB$index$PX_TAB$oom"
    column="$(sb_schema_index oom oom)" || column=1
    kill="$(px_tsv_field "$column" "$oom")" || kill=""
  else
    record_row skip post "out-of-memory evidence of $role/$index" "exit $status"
  fi
  px_error "instance $role/$index: termination reason ${reason:-not known}," \
    "out-of-memory kill ${kill:-not known}"
}

# Compares every instance between the pre-run and post-run snapshots. An instance is considered
# changed if its restart count increased, if it was running before and is now exited, dead, or
# unknown, or if it vanished from the inventory. Any change not covered by a planned action of the
# current run constitutes a cluster anomaly (failing the run with exit code 3) and triggers evidence
# collection into the run record.
## @description  Diffs pre-run and post-run instance states and restart counts to detect anomalies.
## @return       Sets ANOMALY=1 on any unexplained instance change. Exit 0.
restart_diff() {
  local line rest role index before after pre_state post_state verdict status
  local changed comparable
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -n "$line" ] || continue
    role=${line%%"$PX_TAB"*}
    rest=${line#*"$PX_TAB"}
    index=${rest%%"$PX_TAB"*}
    rest=${rest#*"$PX_TAB"}
    pre_state=${rest%%"$PX_TAB"*}
    before=${rest#*"$PX_TAB"}
    status=0
    rest="$(snapshot_lookup "$POST_RESTARTS" "$role" "$index")" || status=$?
    if [ "$status" != 0 ]; then
      if planned_covers "$role" "$index"; then
        record_row diff "$role" "$index" "$before" "" planned "$pre_state" ""
      else
        ANOMALY=1
        px_error "instance $role/$index vanished with no planned action"
        record_row diff "$role" "$index" "$before" "" vanished "$pre_state" ""
        evidence_record "$role" "$index"
      fi
      continue
    fi
    post_state=${rest%%"$PX_TAB"*}
    after=${rest#*"$PX_TAB"}
    # Restart counts that are empty or non-decimal cannot be compared numerically; the raw values
    # are preserved in the diff record so that the reporting fault remains visible.
    comparable=1
    case "$before" in '' | *[!0-9]*) comparable=0 ;; esac
    case "$after" in '' | *[!0-9]*) comparable=0 ;; esac
    changed=""
    if [ "$comparable" = 1 ] && [ "$after" -gt "$before" ]; then
      changed="restarted $before to $after"
    fi
    if [ "$pre_state" = running ]; then
      case "$post_state" in
      exited | dead | unknown)
        changed="${changed:+$changed and }went from running to $post_state"
        ;;
      esac
    fi
    if [ -n "$changed" ]; then
      if planned_covers "$role" "$index"; then
        verdict=planned
      else
        verdict=unplanned
        ANOMALY=1
        px_error "instance $role/$index $changed with no planned action"
      fi
    elif [ "$comparable" = 0 ]; then
      verdict=unknown
    else
      verdict=unchanged
    fi
    record_row diff "$role" "$index" "$before" "$after" "$verdict" "$pre_state" \
      "$post_state"
    if [ "$verdict" = unplanned ]; then
      evidence_record "$role" "$index"
    fi
  done <<EOF
$PRE_RESTARTS
EOF
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disruption scheduling and injection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Because Bash 3.2 lacks associative arrays, the disruption schedule is stored across seven
# parallel indexed arrays where each index represents a single scheduled disruption entry.

# Validates the specification up front so that px-run fails closed before performing any cluster
# operations; a malformed disruption specification must halt execution immediately rather than
# silently resulting in an uninjected fault.
## @description  Parses, validates, and appends a single disruption specification to the schedule.
## @param        specification
## @return       Exit 0, or 2 if the specification is invalid.
disrupt_add() {
  local spec=$1 when action role index argument extra kind value
  IFS=, read -r when action role index argument extra <<EOF
$spec
EOF
  # Strip leading and trailing whitespace from each parsed field before validation so that
  # specifications formatted with spaces after commas are accepted cleanly.
  px_trim_var when
  px_trim_var action
  px_trim_var role
  px_trim_var index
  px_trim_var argument
  if [ -n "${extra:-}" ]; then
    px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' holds more than five fields"
  fi
  case "${when:-}" in
  +*)
    kind=offset
    value=${when#+}
    [[ $value =~ $PX_RE_INT ]] ||
      px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' needs whole seconds after the plus sign"
    ;;
  phase:*)
    kind=phase
    value=${when#phase:}
    [[ $value =~ ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$ ]] ||
      px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' names an invalid phase '$value'"
    ;;
  *)
    px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' needs +SECONDS or phase:NAME"
    ;;
  esac
  case "${action:-}" in
  kill | signal | restart) ;;
  *) px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' needs the action kill, signal or restart" ;;
  esac
  [[ ${role:-} =~ ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$ ]] ||
    px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' names an invalid role '${role:-}'"
  [[ ${index:-} =~ $PX_RE_INT ]] ||
    px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' needs a whole instance index"
  argument=${argument:-}
  case "$action" in
  kill)
    [ -n "$argument" ] || argument=hard
    sb_valid_value kill_mode "$argument" ||
      px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' names an unknown kill mode '$argument'"
    ;;
  signal)
    [[ $argument =~ ^(SIG)?[A-Z][A-Z0-9]+$ ]] ||
      px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' needs a signal name, for example QUIT"
    ;;
  restart)
    [ -z "$argument" ] ||
      px_usage_error "disruption '$spec' takes no argument after the index"
    ;;
  esac
  DISRUPT_KIND[DISRUPT_COUNT]=$kind
  DISRUPT_WHEN[DISRUPT_COUNT]=$value
  DISRUPT_ACTION[DISRUPT_COUNT]=$action
  DISRUPT_ROLE[DISRUPT_COUNT]=$role
  DISRUPT_INDEX[DISRUPT_COUNT]=$index
  DISRUPT_ARG[DISRUPT_COUNT]=$argument
  DISRUPT_STATE[DISRUPT_COUNT]=pending
  DISRUPT_COUNT=$((DISRUPT_COUNT + 1))
}

# Individual specifications are separated by semicolons so that a complete disruption schedule can
# be passed within a single environment variable or option value.
## @description  Splits and parses a semicolon-delimited disruption schedule string.
## @param        text
## @return       Exit 0, or 2 if any specification in the schedule is invalid.
disrupt_parse() {
  local rest=${1:-} spec
  while [ -n "$rest" ]; do
    case "$rest" in
    *';'*)
      spec=${rest%%;*}
      rest=${rest#*;}
      ;;
    *)
      spec=$rest
      rest=""
      ;;
    esac
    px_trim_var spec
    [ -n "$spec" ] || continue
    disrupt_add "$spec"
  done
}

# In a dry-run plan all entries appear in the pending state, whereas at the end of an active run
# each row reflects the final outcome of that disruption.
## @description  Emits a disrupt record row for every scheduled entry and its current state.
## @return       Exit 0.
disrupt_rows() {
  local index=0
  while [ "$index" -lt "$DISRUPT_COUNT" ]; do
    record_row disrupt "$((index + 1))" "${DISRUPT_KIND[$index]}" \
      "${DISRUPT_WHEN[$index]}" "${DISRUPT_ACTION[$index]}" \
      "${DISRUPT_ROLE[$index]}" "${DISRUPT_INDEX[$index]}" \
      "${DISRUPT_ARG[$index]}" "${DISRUPT_STATE[$index]}"
    index=$((index + 1))
  done
}

# Because signal and restart are optional substrate operations, backend capabilities are checked
# first and unsupported operations are recorded as skipped. The planned action is written to the
# substrate contract record before injecting the fault so that the post-run anomaly check can
# account for it; if recording the planned action fails, the fault is aborted to avoid triggering a
# false cluster anomaly. Conversely, once recorded, an attempted fault remains marked as planned
# even if the backend call reports an error, because the target instance may still have been
# affected and a false anomaly report is worse than a soft one.
## @description  Executes a scheduled disruption through the substrate contract and logs the result.
## @param        index (into the disruption schedule)
## @return       Exit 0.
disrupt_fire() {
  local index=$1 action role instance argument out="" status=0 state line
  action=${DISRUPT_ACTION[$index]}
  role=${DISRUPT_ROLE[$index]}
  instance=${DISRUPT_INDEX[$index]}
  argument=${DISRUPT_ARG[$index]}
  # Mark the entry as no longer pending immediately so that a failed attempt cannot be re-fired.
  DISRUPT_STATE[index]=fail
  px_info "disruption $((index + 1)): $action $role/$instance ${argument:-(no argument)}"
  case "$action" in
  signal)
    sb_capability signal_delivery >/dev/null || status=$PX_EXIT_UNSUPPORTED
    ;;
  restart)
    sb_capability drain_restart >/dev/null || status=$PX_EXIT_UNSUPPORTED
    ;;
  esac
  if [ "$status" = 0 ]; then
    sb_planned_record "$SCOPE" "$action" "$role" "$instance" \
      "run $RUN_ID${argument:+ $argument}" || status=$?
    if [ "$status" != 0 ]; then
      px_error "disruption $((index + 1)) not injected: its planned action" \
        "cannot be recorded, exit $status"
    else
      case "$action" in
      kill)
        out="$(sb_call sb_kill "$SCOPE" "$role" "$instance" "$argument")" ||
          status=$?
        ;;
      signal)
        out="$(sb_call sb_signal "$SCOPE" "$role" "$instance" "$argument")" ||
          status=$?
        ;;
      restart)
        out="$(sb_call sb_restart_drain "$SCOPE" "$role" "$instance" \
          "${PX_TIMEOUT_DRAIN:-300}" "${PX_TIMEOUT_READY:-300}")" || status=$?
        ;;
      esac
    fi
  fi
  case "$status" in
  0) state=ok ;;
  "$PX_EXIT_UNSUPPORTED") state=skip ;;
  *) state=fail ;;
  esac
  if [ "$state" = skip ]; then
    px_warn "disruption $((index + 1)) skipped: the backend cannot $action"
  fi
  DISRUPT_STATE[index]=$state
  record_row action "$(px_epoch_ms)" "$action" "$role" "$instance" "$argument" \
    "$state" "$status"
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -n "$line" ] || continue
    record_line "phase$PX_TAB$role$PX_TAB$instance$PX_TAB$line"
  done <<EOF
$out
EOF
}

## @description  Fires all pending offset-based disruptions whose scheduled time has arrived.
## @param        now (current epoch time in seconds)
## @return       Exit 0.
disrupt_due() {
  local now=$1 index=0
  while [ "$index" -lt "$DISRUPT_COUNT" ]; do
    if [ "${DISRUPT_STATE[$index]}" = pending ] &&
      [ "${DISRUPT_KIND[$index]}" = offset ] &&
      [ "$now" -ge $((DRIVER_START_S + DISRUPT_WHEN[index])) ]; then
      disrupt_fire "$index"
    fi
    index=$((index + 1))
  done
}

# Any disruption that remains pending at the end of the run is explicitly marked as skipped along
# with the reason it did not fire, ensuring that an uninjected fault never makes the post-run
# restart comparison appear clean under false pretenses.
## @description  Finalizes the disruption schedule by recording any unfired entries as skipped.
## @return       Exit 0.
disrupt_sweep() {
  local index=0 reason
  while [ "$index" -lt "$DISRUPT_COUNT" ]; do
    if [ "${DISRUPT_STATE[$index]}" = pending ]; then
      if [ "${DISRUPT_KIND[$index]}" = offset ]; then
        reason="the driver ended before offset ${DISRUPT_WHEN[$index]}s"
      else
        reason="phase ${DISRUPT_WHEN[$index]} was not reached"
      fi
      px_warn "disruption $((index + 1)) did not fire: $reason"
      record_row skip run "disruption $((index + 1))" "$reason"
      DISRUPT_STATE[index]=skip
    fi
    index=$((index + 1))
  done
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dry-run execution plan
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Resolves and validates the output root directory so that the dry-run output guarantees a legal
# destination path, without creating the run directory itself or invoking any backend operations.
## @description  Emits the dry-run execution plan describing what a real run would perform.
## @return       Writes plan rows to stdout. Exit 0.
plan_print() {
  local root
  # Because px_out_root terminates inside a command substitution when the output root is invalid,
  # propagate its non-zero exit status directly to terminate px-run.
  root="$(px_out_root)" || exit "$?"
  record_row plan run_id "$RUN_ID"
  record_row plan run_dir "$root/runs/$RUN_ID"
  record_row plan run_class "${PX_RUN_CLASS:-}"
  record_row plan backend "${PX_BACKEND:-the default of the contract}"
  record_row plan scope "${SCOPE:-none, snapshots skipped}"
  record_row plan driver "${DRIVER_PATH:-none, a real run needs one}"
  record_row plan driver_arguments "${#DRIVER_ARGS[@]}"
  record_row plan driver_timeout_s "$DRIVER_TIMEOUT"
  record_row plan snapshot_pre "sb_list sb_restart_count sb_version_info sb_sample_resources"
  record_row plan disruptions "$DISRUPT_COUNT"
  disrupt_rows
  record_row plan snapshot_post "sb_list sb_restart_count sb_version_info sb_sample_resources"
  record_row plan diff "an unplanned restart, exit or loss gives exit $PX_EXIT_ANOMALY"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Command-line argument parsing and configuration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Because command-line arguments have the highest configuration precedence, --set pairs are
# registered with the cli origin before loading configuration files.
## @description  Applies all NAME=VALUE pairs passed via --set as command-line configuration values.
## @return       Exit 0, or 2 if a variable name is invalid.
apply_set_pairs() {
  local index=0 pair
  while [ "$index" -lt "${#SET_PAIRS[@]}" ]; do
    pair=${SET_PAIRS[$index]}
    px_config_set "${pair%%=*}" "${pair#*=}" cli
    index=$((index + 1))
  done
}

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
  -h | --help)
    usage
    exit "$PX_EXIT_OK"
    ;;
  -d | --driver)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    DRIVER_SPEC=$2
    shift 2
    ;;
  -s | --scope)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    px_config_set PX_SCOPE_ID "$2" cli
    shift 2
    ;;
  -b | --backend)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    px_config_set PX_BACKEND "$2" cli
    shift 2
    ;;
  -c | --class)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    px_config_set PX_RUN_CLASS "$2" cli
    shift 2
    ;;
  -D | --disrupt)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    if [ -n "$DISRUPT_INPUT" ]; then
      DISRUPT_INPUT="$DISRUPT_INPUT;$2"
    else
      DISRUPT_INPUT=$2
    fi
    shift 2
    ;;
  -t | --timeout)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    [[ $2 =~ $PX_RE_INT ]] || px_usage_error "$1 needs a whole number of seconds"
    px_config_set PX_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT "$2" cli
    shift 2
    ;;
  -e | --set)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    case "$2" in
    *=*) SET_PAIRS[${#SET_PAIRS[@]}]=$2 ;;
    *) px_usage_error "$1 needs NAME=VALUE, got '$2'" ;;
    esac
    shift 2
    ;;
  -f | --config)
    px_cli_need_value "$@"
    CONFIG_FILES[${#CONFIG_FILES[@]}]=$2
    shift 2
    ;;
  -n | --dry-run)
    px_config_set PX_DRY_RUN 1 cli
    shift
    ;;
  -k | --keep)
    px_config_set PX_KEEP 1 cli
    shift
    ;;
  -v | --verbose)
    px_config_set PX_VERBOSE 1 cli
    shift
    ;;
  --)
    shift
    DRIVER_ARGS=("$@")
    break
    ;;
  -*) px_usage_error "unknown option $1" ;;
  *) px_usage_error "px-run takes no positional argument, got '$1'" ;;
  esac
done

# The configuration loader preserves the first value assigned to each variable; register all
# command-line overrides first so that the highest-precedence origin wins.
if [ -n "$DRIVER_SPEC" ]; then
  px_config_set PX_DRIVER "$DRIVER_SPEC" cli
fi
if [ -n "$DISRUPT_INPUT" ]; then
  px_config_set PX_DISRUPT_SPEC "$DISRUPT_INPUT" cli
fi
apply_set_pairs
px_config_load ${CONFIG_FILES[@]+"${CONFIG_FILES[@]}"}

# Resolve the target scope from PX_SCOPE_ID or from the scope recorded by px-cluster for the active
# cluster. Running without a provisioned cluster is a supported mode that skips cluster snapshots;
# catching the exit status inside the command substitution prevents the error trap from firing when
# no cluster object exists.
SCOPE_STATUS=0
SCOPE="$(sb_scope_resolve || exit "$?")" || SCOPE_STATUS=$?
case "$SCOPE_STATUS" in
0) ;;
"$PX_EXIT_NO_OBJECT") SCOPE="" ;;
*) px_die "$SCOPE_STATUS" "cannot resolve the scope of cluster ${PX_CLUSTER_ID:-default}" ;;
esac
DRIVER_SPEC=${PX_DRIVER:-}
DRIVER_TIMEOUT=${PX_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT:-3600}
[[ $DRIVER_TIMEOUT =~ $PX_RE_INT ]] ||
  px_usage_error "PX_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT needs whole seconds, got '$DRIVER_TIMEOUT'"
if [ -n "$DRIVER_SPEC" ]; then
  DRIVER_PATH="$(driver_resolve "$DRIVER_SPEC")" || exit "$?"
fi
disrupt_parse "${PX_DISRUPT_SPEC:-}"
if [ "$DISRUPT_COUNT" -gt 0 ] && [ -z "$SCOPE" ]; then
  px_usage_error "a disruption needs a cluster: run px-cluster up, or name one" \
    "with --scope"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dry-run termination: print the execution plan and exit without invoking the
# backend or writing anything beyond the validated output root and state directory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if px_is_dry_run; then
  RUN_ID="$(px_run_id)"
  plan_print
  exit "$PX_EXIT_OK"
fi

if [ -z "$DRIVER_PATH" ]; then
  px_usage_error "a run needs a driver: name one with --driver or PX_DRIVER"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test run execution
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

RUN_DIR="$(px_run_dir)" || exit "$?"
RUN_ID=${RUN_DIR##*/}
RECORD_FILE="$RUN_DIR/run-record.tsv"
DRIVER_LOG="$RUN_DIR/driver.log"
# Because px-run mints PX_RUN_ID and PX_RUN_DIR for each execution, it exports them directly rather
# than passing them through the configuration loader, whose first-writer-wins rule is designed for
# operator-supplied inputs rather than per-run generated outputs.
PX_RUN_ID=$RUN_ID
PX_RUN_DIR=$RUN_DIR
export PX_RUN_ID PX_RUN_DIR
printf '%s\n' "$RUN_DIR"
px_info "run $RUN_ID in $RUN_DIR"

sb_load
px_on_exit driver_terminate

record_row run "$RUN_ID" "${PX_RUN_CLASS:-}" "$(sb_bound_backend)" "$SCOPE" "$DRIVER_PATH"
# Open the planned-action timestamp window before reaching the first phase that can trigger a
# disruption so that every fault injected during this run falls within the window.
PLANNED_SINCE_MS="$(px_epoch_ms)"
phase_reached setup

if [ -n "$SCOPE" ]; then
  SCOPE_STATUS=0
  sb_call sb_scope_exists "$SCOPE" || SCOPE_STATUS=$?
  if [ "$SCOPE_STATUS" != 0 ]; then
    px_die "$SCOPE_STATUS" "the scope $SCOPE is not usable"
  fi
  snapshot_take pre || px_die "$PX_EXIT_FAIL" "the run needs a pre-run snapshot"
else
  px_warn "no scope id and no recorded cluster: px-run takes no snapshot and" \
    "checks no instance"
  record_row skip pre scope "no scope id"
fi
phase_reached snapshot_pre

driver_start
phase_reached driver_start
driver_watch
DRIVER_STATUS=0
{ wait "$DRIVER_PID"; } 2>/dev/null || DRIVER_STATUS=$?
DRIVER_PID=""
record_row driver "$(($(px_epoch_ms) - DRIVER_START_MS))" "$DRIVER_STATUS" \
  "$DRIVER_LOG"
px_info "driver ended with status $DRIVER_STATUS, output in $DRIVER_LOG"
phase_reached driver_exit

if [ -n "$SCOPE" ]; then
  # An inability to read the post-run inventory does not prove that instances vanished, so run the
  # state and restart comparison only when the post-run snapshot succeeds.
  if snapshot_take post; then
    restart_diff
  else
    SNAPSHOT_FAILED=1
    record_row skip post diff "the post-run inventory cannot be read"
  fi
else
  record_row skip post scope "no scope id"
fi
phase_reached snapshot_post
disrupt_sweep
disrupt_rows

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Final run verdict
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The workload verdict belongs to the driver, so its exit code passes through directly whenever it
# falls within the shared exit-code vocabulary. Any driver exit status outside that vocabulary
# (such as 143 from a termination signal) is normalized to a general failure (exit code 1), while
# the run record preserves the raw numeric status emitted by the driver.

EXIT_CODE=$DRIVER_STATUS
if [ "$DRIVER_TIMED_OUT" = 1 ]; then
  EXIT_CODE=$PX_EXIT_TIMEOUT
else
  case "$EXIT_CODE" in
  0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6) ;;
  "$SB_EXEC_TIMEOUT") EXIT_CODE=$PX_EXIT_TIMEOUT ;;
  *)
    px_warn "the driver returned $DRIVER_STATUS, which is outside the vocabulary"
    EXIT_CODE=$PX_EXIT_FAIL
    ;;
  esac
fi
if [ "$ANOMALY" = 1 ] && { [ "$EXIT_CODE" = 0 ] || [ "$EXIT_CODE" = 1 ]; }; then
  EXIT_CODE=$PX_EXIT_ANOMALY
fi
if [ "$EXIT_CODE" = 0 ] && [ "$SNAPSHOT_FAILED" = 1 ]; then
  px_error "the post-run snapshot failed, so the run cannot be reported clean"
  EXIT_CODE=$PX_EXIT_FAIL
fi

if [ "$ANOMALY" = 1 ]; then
  record_row result "$EXIT_CODE" "$(px_exit_name "$EXIT_CODE")" yes
else
  record_row result "$EXIT_CODE" "$(px_exit_name "$EXIT_CODE")" no
fi
px_info "run $RUN_ID ends with exit $EXIT_CODE, $(px_exit_name "$EXIT_CODE")"
exit "$EXIT_CODE"
